      "id": "chapter-12",
      "number": 12,
      "title": "Chapter 12 \u2014 Founder Operating Templates and Scorecards",
      "paragraphs": [
        "12.1 CODE opportunity scorecard\nScore each dimension from 1 (weak evidence) to 5 (strong evidence). Add a short explanation and source for every score.\n\n| Dimension | Score (1-5) | Evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Consumer trend is real and growing | | |\n| Specific opportunity exists | | |\n| Customers actively discuss the problem | | |\n| Current alternatives show spending or effort | | |\n| Buyer and budget are identifiable | | |\n| Market economics are plausible | | |\n| Team can reach and serve the ICP | | |\n\nDo not let a high total hide a fatal weakness. For example, a large market does not compensate for the absence of a buyer or an inability to reach customers.",
        "12.2 Customer interview record\nUse this template for customer discovery calls:\n- Customer / organisation:\n- Role:\n- Date:\n- Current workflow:\n- Trigger for the problem:\n- Most painful step:\n- Current tools or alternatives:\n- Time or money spent:\n- Consequence of doing nothing:\n- Who approves a purchase:\n- Exact phrases used by the customer:\n- Evidence observed:\n- Unanswered questions:\n- Next step agreed:",
        "12.3 MVP scope filter\nFor every proposed feature, ask:\n1. Does it help the first ICP complete the core job?\n2. Is it necessary for the first useful result?\n3. Is there evidence that users need it now?\n4. Can we deliver the value with a simpler workflow?\n5. Does it create significant security, data, or reliability risk?\n6. What will we delay if we build it?\n\nIf the feature is not essential to the first value moment, move it out of the MVP.",
        "12.4 Weekly founder review\nReview these questions every week:\n\nCustomer:\n- What did we learn directly from customers?\n- Which problem appeared repeatedly?\n- What evidence contradicts our assumptions?\n\nProduct:\n- Did users reach the core value moment?\n- Where did they get stuck?\n- What did we ship in response?\n\nDistribution:\n- Which content attracted the right audience?\n- Which outreach created meaningful conversations?\n- Which channel generated qualified leads?\n\nRevenue:\n- How many customers paid?\n- What objections prevented conversion?\n- Are customers renewing or expanding?\n\nTeam:\n- Are responsibilities clear?\n- What is the current bottleneck?\n- What should we stop doing?",
        "12.5 Metrics: distinguish the numbers\n\n| Metric | Meaning | Common mistake |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ARR | Annualised recurring revenue run rate | Treating it as cash collected or profit |\n| MRR | Monthly recurring revenue | Confusing it with one-time sales |\n| Bookings | Contracted value over a defined period | Assuming all bookings are immediately recognised revenue |\n| GMV | Total value transacted through a marketplace | Treating it as the platform's revenue |\n| Pipeline | Potential deals at different sales stages | Treating unclosed deals as revenue |\n| Valuation | Estimated value of the company in a financing context | Treating it as liquid founder wealth |\n| Activation | A user reaches a defined first-value event | Counting every signup as an active user |\n| Retention | Customers continue using or paying over time | Looking only at new-customer growth |\n\nFor a subscription business, a useful early dashboard includes new MRR, expansion MRR, contraction MRR, churned MRR, customer count, activation, retention, acquisition source, and support burden. Define every metric consistently."
      ]
